    "brief": {
      "label": "Kessler",
      "title": "Counter Kessler at +3.0% against a +2.9% should-cost move",
      "meta": "Brief SUP-0184-K · prepared for Sourcer · response due Oct 4 · internal only",
      "sections": [
        {
          "head": "Situation",
          "body": "Kessler gave formal notice of +8.0% across 14 SKUs effective Jan 1, using the 90-day clause correctly. They are our largest supplier at 31% of spend, single-source on four SKUs, and $374K of their POs are in flight. They have also been below the Tier 1 OTD floor for two quarters."
        },
        {
          "head": "Should-cost basis",
          "body": "Rebuilding their basket from published indices gives +2.9%: steel +4.1% at 38% weight, labour +3.2% at 22%, energy +1.8% at 9%, offset by the Ningbo–LA lane at −6.2% on 19% of input cost. Overhead and margin held flat per contract. The unexplained gap is 5.1pp, or $44.9K a year at current volume."
        },
        {
          "head": "Opening move",
          "body": "Lead with the build-up, not the counter-number. Ask them to walk their own cost breakdown against the same indices — they provided the input mix in March, so this is their arithmetic, not ours. The freight line is the one they will not have accounted for."
        },
        {
          "head": "What we can offer",
          "body": "Volume they have never had committed: 34% YoY growth, currently ordered PO by PO. An 18-month commitment at the new volume is worth more to their planning than the 5.1pp is worth to their margin, and it costs us nothing we were not going to spend."
        },
        {
          "head": "If they refuse",
          "body": "Nanjing Ironworks quotes −1.8% on all four single-source SKUs at 14 weeks to qualify, and Hoyt Forge covers two of them domestically in 8 weeks at +4.2%. Dual-sourcing 40% is a live option, not a bluff — it is costed at $63K including the safety-stock build."
        }
      ],
